NTSYNC + userspace ntsync support for Wine 11.17 (Termux/Hangover).

Ported from GameNative/proton-wine (android/patches/common/*ntsync* and
related commits d67ac1e, 0971187, 962a379) to upstream Wine 11.17, which
removed esync entirely in favour of the ntsync-based in-process sync.

What this patch does:
- vendors the ntsync-android C API headers (android/ntsync_android/):
  ntsync_kernel_abi.h mirrors the frozen /dev/ntsync ioctl ABI and
  ntsync_user.h declares the userspace (libntsync_android) API, so both
  backends compile on Android without the kernel uapi header.
- wineserver (server/inproc_sync.c): runtime backend detection - it opens
  /dev/ntsync and probes it with a real NTSYNC_IOC_CREATE_EVENT ioctl
  (catches SELinux/seccomp-blocked nodes), falls back to the userspace
  ntsync-android shm region, and honours PROTON_NO_NTSYNC (disable both)
  and PROTON_NO_KERNEL_NTSYNC (force userspace) env switches.
- protocol (protocol.def + regenerated request_handlers.h /
  request_trace.h / server_protocol.h, version 962): the
  get_inproc_sync_fd / get_inproc_alert_fd replies gain an ntsync_handle
  field used to pass userspace object handles instead of SCM_RIGHTS fds.
- ntdll (unix/sync.c, unix/server.c): attaches to the shared region when
  the server reports NTSYNC_ANDROID_USED_BY_SERVER, dispatches every
  semaphore/event/mutex operation and wait to the userspace backend,
  and supports alertable waits through the per-thread alert event.
- server/process.c: reclaims objects of dead processes
  (ntsync_sweep_dead) - userspace ntsync has no fd-close-on-death hook.
- ntdll + wineserver link the static libntsync_android.a (-lntsync_android);
  it is built from https://github.com/GameNative/ntsync-android by
  build.sh (termux_setup_rust + cargo build --release, staticlib).

Differences from the GameNative original: upstream 11.17 has no fsync, so
all do_fsync()/fsync_shm_idx code paths are dropped; the new reply field
is named ntsync_handle instead of reusing Proton's fsync_shm_idx; the
GameNative container dosdevices mapping is not ported (termux-fix-paths
handles dosdevices for Termux).

Co-developed with the GameNative/proton-wine patches by Joshua Tam
(ntsync-android library is LGPL-3.0-only, headers keep their license).
